# {{lb|en|transitive}}
## To [[give#Verb|give]] [[power#Noun|power]], [[strength#Noun|strength]], or [[vigour]] to (oneself or someone, or to something); to [[strengthen]].
##* {{quote-book|en|author=w:William Bullein|chapter=The Booke of Compoundes|title=Bulleins Bulwarke of Defence against All Sicknesse, Soarenesse, and Woundes that Doo Dayly Assaulte Mankinde:{{nb...|Which Bulwarke is Kept with Hilarius the Gardener, & Health the Physicion, with the Chirurgian, to Helpe the Wounded Souldiours. Gathered and Practised from the Most Worthy Learned, both Olde and New: To the Great Comfort of Mankinde: By William Bullein, Doctor of Phisicke. 1562.}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Imprinted at London by}} Thomas Marshe,{{nb...|dwellinge in Fleetestreate neare vnto Saincte Dunstanes Church}}|year=1562|year_published=1579|section=folio 15, verso|sectionurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=NQc2AQAAMAAJ&pg=SL177-PA48-IA31|oclc=606503690|passage=[I]t [“oleum de costo”] '''fortifyeth''' y&#868; ſtomack and Lyuer, it keepeth the hayre from fallyng of, & the head from horeneſſe [hoaryness], it cauſeth good colour, and ſauour in all the body.}}
##* {{RQ:Boyle New Experiments|version=C1|chapter=Experiment XXXII. Shewing, that when the Pressure of the External Air is Taken off, tis Very Easie to Draw up the Sucker of a Syringe, though the Hole, at which the Air or Water should Succeed, be Stopp’d.|page=107|passage={{...}} I preſum'd it vvill not be unvvelcome to Your Lordſhip, if I here '''fortifie''' the Speculations that have been or may be propos'd to explicate theſe things according to the ''Hypotheſis'' of the vveight of the Air, by vvhat vve tried to that purpoſe, among others, vvhen vve vvere making uſe of a Syringe in our Engine.}}
##* {{RQ:Swift Nobles and Commons|page=8|passage=[T]hey admitted three thouſand into a ſhare of the Government; and thus '''fortified''', became the cruelleſt Tyranny upon Record.}}
##* {{RQ:Spectator|author=Addison|issue=121|date=19 July 1711|page=144|passage=Nor must we here omit that great variety of arms with which Nature has differently '''fortified''' the bodies of several kinds of animals; such as claws, hoofs, horns, teeth, and tusks, a tail, a sting, a trunk, or a proboscis.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|translator=[[w:William Kay (scholar)|W[illiam] Kay]]|chapter=[[w:Psalm 138|Psalm CXXXVIII]] [Verse 3]|title=[[w:Psalms|The Psalms, Translated from the Hebrew,{{nb...|with Notes Chiefly Critical and Exegetical}}]]|location=Calcutta, West Bengal|publisher=R. C. Lepage and Co.|year=1863|page=304|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=XOhUAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA304|oclc=85001003|passage=In the day I called, Thou [God] answeredst me; / Thou '''fortifiedst''' me with strength in my soul.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:Émile Souvestre|translator=A. W. C.|title=What One Learns Looking Out of a Window|editor=[Roderick] Noble|journal=[[w:Cape Monthly Magazine|The Cape Monthly Magazine]]|location=Cape Town, Cape Colony|publisher=J. C. Juta|month=February|year=1873|volume=VI (New Series)|page=96|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=jk5TAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA96|oclc=10281727|passage=Thou [poverty] '''fortifiest''' the body, thou strengthenest the soul; and thanks to thee, this life to which the rich cling to as a rock, becomes as a skiff whose anchor death can part without awakening our despair.}}
## To [[support#Verb|support]] (one's or someone's [[opinion#Noun|opinion]], [[statement#Noun|statement]], etc.) by [[produce#Verb|producing]] [[evidence#Noun|evidence]], etc.; to [[confirm#Adjective|confirm]], to [[corroborate]].
##* {{RQ:Thomas More Workes|title=Heresyes|book=I|chapter=XXVI|page=164|column=1|passage=And vnto that texte he wold haue made you a [[gloss|gloſe]], that his father and he were one not in ſubſtaunce but in will. And that gloſe he would haue '''fortified''' and made ſomewhat ſemely with an other worde of Chriſt, {{...}}}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Thomas Fitzherbert|chapter=VII|title=The Obmutesce of F. T. to the Epphata of D. [[w:Samuel Collins (theologian)|[Samuel] Collins]]. Or The Reply of F. T. to D. Collins His Defence of My Lord of Winchesters Answere to Cardinall [[w:Robert Bellarmine|[Robert] Bellarmines]] Apology.{{nb...|In which Reply M. Collins is Conuinced of Most Manifest Frauds, Falsityes, Fooleryes, & Lyes. Written by Thomas Fitzherbert Priest of the Society of Iesus, in Defence of His Adioynder Impugned by M. Collins: Wherein the Authors Name was Cyphred with the Two Letters F. T.}}|location=[Saint-Omer, France]|publisher=[English College Press]|year=1621|section=paragraph 44|pages=211–212|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=FlbgdZ3hvysC&pg=PA212|oclc=55581108|passage=[I]t may ſerue for no ſmal iuſtification of the tranſlatour that he '''fortifyeth''' his tranſlation vvith the authority of ſuch an auncient manuſcript, vvritten aboue eleuen hundred yeares ago, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Emerson Representative Men|page=58|passage=If he made transcendental distinctions, he '''fortified''' himself by drawing all his illustrations from sources disdained by orators and polite conversers; from mares and puppies; from pitchers and soup-ladles; from cooks and criers; the shops of potters, horse-doctors, butchers, and fishmongers.}}
## To [[increase#Verb|increase]] the [[nutritional]] [[value#Noun|value]] of ([[food]]) by [[add#Verb|adding]] [[ingredient]]s, especially [[mineral#Noun|minerals]] or [[vitamin]]s. {{defdate|from mid 20th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|Soy milk is often '''fortified''' with calcium.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|title=What’s in Cereals|editor=Sidney Sulkin|journal=[[w:Kiplinger's Personal Finance|Changing Times: The Kiplinger Magazine]]|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher={{w|Austin H. Kiplinger}}, Kiplinger Washington Editors|month=July|year=1979|volume=33|issue=7|page=47|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=BwQEAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA47|column=1|issn=0009-143X|oclc=1334715686|passage=Compare the nutrition information label of a regular ready-to-eat '''fortified''' cereal with that of a presweetened brand and you'll note that, although the sweetened one's sugar content is higher, the fortification is virtually identical.|footer={{small|An adjective use.}}}}
## {{lb|en|figurative}}
### To [[impart]] [[fortitude]] or [[moral#Adjective|moral]] strength to (someone or their [[determination]], or something); to [[encourage]].
###* {{RQ:Sidney Arcadia|chapter=4|folio=117|passage=[H]er ovvne proofe taught her to knovv her mothers minde; vvhich {{...}} greatly '''fortified''' her deſires, to ſee, that her mother had the like deſires. And the more iealous her mother was, the more ſhe thought the Ievvell precious, vvhich vvas vvith ſo many lookes garded.}}
###* {{RQ:Ray Wisdom|chapter=Epistle Dedicatory|page=11|passage={{...}} I had rather vvrite of you to others, to provoke them to imitate ſo excellent an Example, than to your Self, to encourage you in your Chriſtian Courſe, and to '''fortifie''' you in your Athletick Conflicts vvith the greateſt of temporal Evils, bodily Pain and Anguiſh; {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Locke Education|edition=3rd|section=70|pages=98–99|pageref=99|passage=A young Man, before he leaves the ſhelter of his Father's Houſe and the guard of a Tutor, ſhould be '''fortified''' vvith Reſolution, and made acquainted vvith Men, to ſecure his Vertue; leſt he ſhould be led into ſome ruinous courſe, or fatal precipice, before he is ſufficiently acquainted vvith the Dangers of Converſation, and has Steadineſs enough not to yield to every Temptation.}}
###* {{RQ:Johnson Rambler|issue=155|date=10 September 1751|page=181|passage=[E]very delay gives vice another opportunity of '''fortifying''' itſelf by habit, and the change of manners, though ſincerely intended and rationally planned, is referred to the time vvhen ſome craving paſſion ſhall be fully gratified, or ſome povverful allurement ceaſe its importunity.}}
###* {{RQ:Hume History|volume=2|chapter=XX|page=355|passage=The princeſs fell immediately into cloſe connexions vvith the cardinal and his party, the dukes of Somerſet, Suffolk, and Buckingham; vvho, '''fortified''' by her povverful friendſhip, reſolved on the final ruin of the duke of Gloceſter.}}
###* {{RQ:Gibbon Miscellaneous Works|volume=I|page=146|passage=I vvas not armed by Nature and education vvith the intrepid energy of mind and voice. {{...}} Timidity vvas '''fortified''' by pride, and even the ſucceſs of my pen diſcouraged the trial of my voice.}}
###* {{RQ:Scott Guy Mannering|volume=I|chapter=XIX|page=302|passage=So pride came to the aid of fancy, and both combined to '''fortify''' his resolution to buy the estate if possible.}}
###* {{RQ:Dickens Our Mutual Friend|volume=I|chapter=Tracking the Bird of Prey|page=123|passage=Mr. Inspector, hastily '''fortifying''' himself with another glass, strolled out with a noiseless foot and an unoccupied countenance.}}
###* {{RQ:Arnold Essays in Criticism|edition=3rd|page=269|passage=He pressed his friends to consult their safety by a timely flight; they unanimously refused to desert or survive their beloved master, and their courage was '''fortified''' by a fervent prayer and the assurance of paradise.}}
###* {{RQ:Kipling Letters of Travel|chapter=Dead Kings|page=261|passage=Even the sight of a very great king indeed, sarcophagused under electric light in a hall full of most '''fortifying''' pictures, does not hold him [a visitor to the Valley of the Kings, Egypt] too long.|footer=An adjective use.}}
###* {{RQ:Wodehouse Jeeves in the Offing|chapter=XXI|passage="And do you realize that in a few shakes I've got to show up at dinner and have Mrs Cream being very, very kind to me? It hurts the pride of the Woosters, Jeeves." / "My advice, sir, would be to '''fortify''' yourself for the ordeal." / "How?" / "There are always cocktails, sir. Should I pour you another?" / "You should."}}
### To make (something) [[defensible]] against [[attack#Noun|attack]] by [[hostile#Adjective|hostile]] [[force#Noun|forces]].
###* {{RQ:Milton Areopagitica|page=16|passage=For if they fell upon one kind of ſtrictneſſe, unleſſe their cure vvere equall to regulat all other things of like aptnes to corrupt the mind, that ſingle endeavour they knevv vvould be but a fond labour; to ſhut and '''fortifie''' one gate againſt corruption, and be neceſſitated to leave others round about vvide open.}}
###* {{RQ:Johnson Taxation|page=31|passage=It is, ſay the American advocates, the natural diſtinction of a freeman, and the legal privilege of an Engliſhman, that he is able to call his poſſeſſions his ovvn, that he can ſit ſecure in the enjoyment of inheritance or acquiſition, that his houſe is '''fortified''' by the lavv, and that nothing can be taken from him but by his ovvn conſent.}}
## {{lb|en|archaic}} To [[make#Verb|make]] (something) [[structurally]] [[strong#Adjective|strong]]; to strengthen.
##* {{RQ:Topsell Foure-footed Beastes|chapter=Of the Lyon|page=478|passage=The third manner of hunting is done vvith leſſer labour: that is, foure ſtrong men armed vvith ſhields, and '''fortified''' all ouer vvith thonges of leather, and hauing helmets vpon their heades, that onely their eyes, noſes, and lips may appeare, vvith the brandiſhing of their firebrands ruſtle in vpon the lyon lying in his den: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Dryden Georgics|book=4|lines=256–257 and 262–263|page=130|passage=If little things vvith great vve may compare, / Such are the Bees, and ſuch their native Care; / {{...}} / To '''fortify''' the Combs, to build the VVall, / To prop the Ruins leſt the Fabrick fall: {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|military}}
### To [[increase#Verb|increase]] the [[defence#Noun|defences]] of (an [[army]], [[soldier#Noun|soldiers]], etc.), or [[put#Verb|put]] (it or them) in a [[defensive#Adjective|defensive]] [[position#Noun|position]].
###* {{RQ:Marlowe Tamburlaine|part=2|scene=ii|sig=G4|page=113|passage=Ile haue you learne to ſleepe vpon the ground, / March in your armor thorow watery fens, / {{...}} / Then next, the way to '''fortifie''' your men, / In champion grounds, {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Irving Rocky Mountains|volume=II|chapter=XI|page=120|passage=They {{...}} descended Snake river again, and encamped just above the American falls. Here they proceeded to '''fortify''' themselves, intending to remain here, and give their horses an opportunity to recruit their strength with good pasturage, until it should be time to set out for the annual rendezvous in Bear river valley.}}
### To [[secure#Verb|secure]] and strengthen (a [[place#Noun|place]], its [[wall#Noun|walls]], etc.) by [[install#Verb|installing]] [[fortification]]s or other [[military#Adjective|military]] [[work#Noun|works]]. {{defdate|from early 15th c.}}
###* {{RQ:Sidney Arcadia|chapter=4|folio=307|passage=But ''Baſilius'' (having bene the last day ſomevvhat vnprouided) novv had better '''fortified''' the ouerthrovvne [[sconce#Etymology 3|ſconſe]]; and so vvell had prepared every thing for defence, that it vvas impoſſible for any valour from vvithin, to prevaile.}}
###* {{RQ:Pliny Holland Historie of the World|tome=1|book=VI|chapter=The Regions and People Confining upon the Scythian Ocean|page=121|passage=Moreover ''Agrippa'' vvriteth, that this vvhole coaſt of Albanie ('''fortified''' vvith thoſe high and inacceſſible mountains of Caucaſus) containeth 425 miles.|footer=A figurative use.}}
###* {{RQ:King James Version|Isaiah|22|10|page=779|column=1|passage=And ye haue numbred the houſes of Jeruſalem, and the houſes haue yee broken downe to '''fortifie''' the wall.}}
###* {{RQ:Addison Italy|chapter=Monaco, Genoa, &c.|page=8|passage=There are but Three Tovvns in the Dominions of the Prince of ''Monaco''. The chief of 'em is ſituate on a Rock that runs out into the Sea, and is vvell '''fortify'd''' by Nature.|footer=A figurative use.}}
###* {{RQ:Robertson History of Scotland|volume=I|book=III|page=189|passage=[B]eſides puniſhing the diſaffection of the inhabitants, by pillaging the country, the French propoſed to ſeize and '''fortify''' St. Andrevv's, and to leave there a garriſon ſufficient to bridle the mutinous ſpirit of the province, and to keep poſſeſſion of a port ſituated on the main ocean.}}
### {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[provide]] (a [[city]], a [[fortress#Noun|fortress]], an army, etc.) with [[equipment]] or soldiers.
###* {{RQ:Defoe New Voyage|part=I|page=29|passage=At the ſame time, I '''fortified''' my ſelf vvith the ''French'' Captain, and the ''Supra Cargo'', and the other Captain; {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|winemaking}} To [[add#Verb|add]] [[spirits#Noun|spirits]] to ([[wine#Noun|wine]]) to increase the [[alcohol]] [[content#Noun|content]]. {{defdate|from late 19th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|Sherry is made by '''fortifying''' wine.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}}
## {{lb|en|military}} To install fortifications or other military works; also {{lb|en|sometimes|figurative}}, to put up a defensive position.
##* {{RQ:Lyly Endymion|scene=iii|sig=C|verso=1|page=13|passage={{...}} I vvill vvithdravv my ſelfe to the Riuer, & there '''fortifie''' for fiſh: for there reſteth no minute free from fight.|footer=A figurative use.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Sonnets|sonnet=63|sig=E2|page=95|passage=For ſuch a time do I novv '''fortifie''' / Againſt confounding Ages cruell knife, / That he ſhall neuer cut from memory / My ſvveet loues beauty, though my louers life.}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[become]] strong; to strengthen.
##* {{RQ:Bacon Learning|book=2|folio=82|passage=But the poets and vvriters of Hiſtories are the beſt Doctors of this knovvledge, vvhere vve may finde painted fourth vvith greate life, Hovv affections are kindled and incyted: and hovv pacified and reſtrained: and hovv againe Conteyned from Act, & furder degree: hovv they diſcloſe themſelues, hovv they vvork hovv they varye, hovv they gather and '''fortifie''', hovv they are invvrapped one vvithin another, a[n]d hovve they doe fighte and encounter one vvith another, and other the like particularityes: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:De Bonnefons Evelyn French Gardiner|section=III|sectionname=Of Trees, and of the Choice which Ought to be Made of Them|page=28|passage=From ''Peare-trees'' grafted upon the free-ſtock you ſhould cut off the ''dovvn-right root'', that ſo the other ''roots'' may '''fortifie''' and extend themſelves all about to ſeeke the beſt mould.}}

